<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parliament of Friuli</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Parlamento della Patria del Friuli</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> was the representative assembly of the Patria del Friuli, the state of the patriarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, documented from the 13th century. It brought together representatives of the clergy, the nobility and the towns, voted taxes and troops and shared in legislation and government with the patriarch. It survived, with reduced powers, under Venetian rule from 1420 until the end of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The parliament grew out of patriarchal councils of increasing size, called in the 13th century to win the money and soldiers of the Friulian barons for the patriarchs' near continual wars with their neighbours. By the end of that century it had become a regular institution, and in the early 14th century it described itself as acting in the name of the whole population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a>. Throughout patriarchal rule its main business was military: barons, churchmen and towns served according to duties listed in the talea militiae, which the parliament itself fixed and revised. The patriarch's chief hold over it lay in appointing the gastalds of the towns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The parliament met up to ten times a year and mattered most during vacancies in the patriarchal office, which were frequent. Plenary sessions never lasted more than a day, and the work was done by committees. One, of sixteen men (two churchmen, two barons, eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ministerialis">ministeriales</a> and four town representatives) with any appointees of the patriarch, met straight after a plenary session to apply its decisions. A second, of three members from each of the three estates and six appointees of the patriarch, sat for six months and became in effect a standing committee.</p><p>The parliament judged cases of feudal law, conspiracy and rebellion and civil suits against the patriarch, and was the court of last resort in almost all cases. In March 1355 it codified a constitution, the Constitutiones Patriae Foriiulii, amended in 1366, 1368 and 1380.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The parliament had no part in the Venetian conquest of 1420, but it went on working with a lieutenant appointed by the Venetian Senate as it had with the patriarch, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> confirmed the constitution in 1429; it remained the fundamental law of the Patria del <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> until the fall of Venice in 1797. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udine">Udine</a> and Cividale stopped taking part, the assembly divided into two for the lands east and west of the Tagliamento, and in the mid-16th century a separate body, the corpo della contadinanza, came to represent the rural districts. The antiquary Count Girolamo di Porcia wrote that Friuli under the patriarchs had had more the form of a republic than of a principality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Patria del Friuli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
